#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "M2")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U3 Module maps",[Compute $"Hom"_ZZ(ZZ slash 6ZZ,ZZ slash 9ZZ)$ as an abelian group.],[A map is determined by the image of one.],[That image must be killed by six.],[The allowable images are $[0],[3],[6]$ modulo nine. Under pointwise addition these form a cyclic group of order three, so the Hom group is $ZZ slash 3ZZ$.],[Identify the group operation as well as the number of maps.]),
 p(2,"E","U7 Exterior powers",[Compute the rank of $wedge^3R^5$ and explain why $wedge^6R^5=0$.],[Count increasing index sets.],[Repeated basis indices force a wedge to vanish.],[The first rank is $binom(5,3)=10$. In six slots drawn from five basis indices, some index repeats, so every basis expansion term vanishes and the sixth exterior power is zero.],[Justify both spanning and the repeated-index argument.]),
 p(3,"C","U1 Splitting fields",[Find the degree of the splitting field of $(x^2-2)(x^2-3)$ over $QQ$.],[Adjoin both square roots.],[Show the second is not in the first quadratic field.],[The splitting field is $QQ(sqrt(2),sqrt(3))$. If $sqrt(3)=a+b sqrt(2)$, squaring forces $a b=0$, and either $a^2=3$ or $b^2=3/2$, impossible for rational $a,b$. Thus each adjoining step has degree two and the total degree is four.],[Prove the relative second degree, not merely an upper bound.]),
 p(4,"C","U8 Noetherianity",[Prove $K[x,y] slash (x^2,x y)$ is Noetherian. Must it be a domain?],[Use Hilbert and quotient preservation.],[The nonzero class of $x$ has square zero.],[Hilbert's theorem makes $K[x,y]$ Noetherian, and its quotient is Noetherian. It is not a domain: the class of $x$ is nonzero because the ideal is generated by degree-two monomials, but its square is zero.],[Separate Noetherianity from absence of zero divisors.]),
 p(5,"A","U5–U6 Local detection of a map",[Prove a module map is an isomorphism if its localization at every maximal ideal is an isomorphism.],[Use its kernel and cokernel.],[Localization is exact, and zero modules can be detected at maximal ideals.],[Exactness identifies localized kernels and cokernels with the kernels and cokernels of the localized maps, so both localize to zero at every maximal ideal. The local zero-detection theorem forces the original kernel and cokernel to be zero. The map is therefore injective and surjective, hence an isomorphism.],[No finite-generation assumption is needed; cite the element-annihilator proof of zero detection.]),
 p(6,"A","U4–U7 Projective trace",[Let $M$ be finite projective and $T(m)=phi(m)v$. Prove $"tr"(T)=phi(v)$ using a dual basis.],[Insert the rank-one formula into the trace definition.],[Apply $phi$ to the dual-basis reconstruction of $v$.],[For a dual basis $(m_i,psi_i)$, $"tr"(T)=sum_i psi_i(phi(m_i)v)=sum_i phi(m_i)psi_i(v)$. Since $v=sum_i psi_i(v)m_i$, applying $phi$ gives exactly this sum. Thus the trace is $phi(v)$, without assuming a global ordinary basis.],[Use scalar commutativity and the correct dual-basis identity.]),
)
